Project Overview:
This repository documents a security testing and hardening task performed on OWASP Juice Shop, a deliberately vulnerable web application. 
The task focuses on identifying and testing common web vulnerabilities using PowerShell commands and applying basic security controls at the application level.
The goal of this task is to understand how vulnerabilities like SQL Injection, Cross-Site Scripting (XSS), and Weak Password Authentication work, and how they can be tested and mitigated.

Objectives:
Configure PowerShell to allow Node.js and npm commands
Install and run OWASP Juice Shop locally
Test authentication vulnerabilities using PowerShell
Verify that weak passwords are accepted (default vulnerable behavior)
Perform SQL Injection and XSS testing
Understand how insecure code causes security issues
Practice secure coding concepts in a controlled environment

Tools & Technologies Used:
OWASP Juice Shop
Node.js & npm
PowerShell (Windows)
Express.js
Validator & bcrypt libraries
curl (PowerShell)

Security Testing Performed:
1. Weak Password Testing:
Tested login endpoint with weak passwords
Verified insecure acceptance of weak credentials

2. SQL Injection Testing:
Used crafted SQL payloads via PowerShell
Tested direct query execution vulnerability

XSS Testing:
Injected JavaScript payloads in login fields
Verified lack of input sanitization

Key Learning Outcomes:
PowerShell execution policy can block npm scripts
Unsanitized SQL queries lead to SQL Injection
Missing input validation allows XSS attacks
Weak password acceptance is a serious security risk
Security testing can be done.

